public static int minPerfectSquareSum(int n) {
    int[] dp = new int[n + 1];
    for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
        dp[i] = i;
        for (int j = 1; j * j <= i; j++) {
            dp[i] = Math.min(dp[i], dp[i - j * j] + 1);
        }
    }
    return dp[n];
}
```
This method uses dynamic programming to find the minimum number of perfect square numbers that sum up to `n`. It uses a 1D array `dp` to store the minimum number of perfect square numbers for each `i` from `1` to `n`. The base case is `dp[0] = 0`, since no perfect square numbers are needed to sum up to `0`.

For each `i` from `1` to `n`, we iterate from `1` to `i` and check if `j * j` is less than or equal to `i`. If it is, we update `dp[i]` to be the minimum of `dp[i]` and `dp[i - j * j] + 1`. The `+ 1` is because we need to add `1` to the number of perfect square numbers needed to sum up to `i - j * j` to get the number of perfect square numbers needed to sum up to `i`.

Finally, we return `dp[n]`, which is the minimum number of perfect square numbers needed to sum up to `n`.

